# PS-11841: heap-use-after-free in the ordered index scan of a partitioned
# MyRocks table through an index that contains a VIRTUAL BLOB generated
# column (originally reported as PS-9844).
#
# Partition_base::read_range_first_in_part() copies each partition's row,
# including the blob pointer of the generated column, into that partition's
# priority-queue buffer. handler::ha_index_read_map() then re-evaluates the
# generated column into the one shared Field_blob, which frees the value that
# the row queued for an earlier partition still points to. The queue merge
# (key_rec_cmp() -> Field_blob::cmp_max()) then reads freed memory when two
# queued rows tie on i2. Only ha_rockspart derives from Partition_base, so
# the InnoDB run below is a reference and is not affected.
#
# This test detects the bug only in an ASAN build, which reports
# heap-use-after-free. A debug build without ASAN passes, because the freed
# memory still holds the old bytes; the row-count and ordering checks below
# are what a debug build can see.

--source include/have_rocksdb.inc

--echo # This test detects the bug only in an ASAN build; a debug build passes.

SET SESSION cte_max_recursion_depth = 1000;

--let $engine = ROCKSDB
--echo # Engine: $engine
--source suite/rocksdb/include/partition_vgcol_blob_ordered_scan.inc

--let $engine = InnoDB
--echo # Engine: $engine
--source suite/rocksdb/include/partition_vgcol_blob_ordered_scan.inc
